From 5b486e35e3e4905589a8225bda511663f564d392 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Toemmsen96 <147745097+Toemmsen96@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 18:45:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] power: supply: Add Silicon Mitus SM5703 fuel gauge driver

The fuel gauge of the Silicon Mitus SM5703 PMIC is a separate I2C
device with 16-bit little-endian registers. It estimates the state of
charge from the battery voltage and current with a battery model held
in volatile memory, so the model is lost whenever the gauge loses power
(e.g. when the removable battery is pulled) and has to be programmed
again by the driver.

Add a driver based on the Samsung downstream sm5703_fuelgauge driver:
 - report status, voltage, open circuit voltage, current, state of
   charge and the gauge temperature
 - program the battery model (OCV and capacity tables, RCE, DTCD, RS,
   mixing and calibration values) from the device tree when the
   FG_OP_STATUS register says the gauge is not initialised, and seed it
   with the initial OCV sampled by the gauge at power up
 - reprogram the gauge if it resets itself at runtime, as the
   downstream driver does
 - use the alert interrupt for a low state of charge alert with a
   threshold exposed as capacity_alert_min

The dynamic temperature compensation of the current calibration and
the RS manual mode switching done by the downstream driver are left out
for now.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
